      "overview": [
        {
          "label": "What it is",
          "text": "Modular Chassis MK2 is the second generation of a drivetrain architecture I originally developed for FIRST Tech Challenge robotics. It is built around four independently removable mecanum wheel modules, each treated as a self-contained mechatronic system."
        },
        {
          "label": "Why I made it",
          "text": "I wanted to push the modularity of my original chassis beyond simple mechanical removal by designing the drivetrain, electrical connections, sensing, diagnostics, and cable management around serviceability from the beginning."
        },
        {
          "label": "What I designed",
          "text": "I am designing the complete chassis architecture and its supporting subsystems, including the removable wheel modules, gearbox and wheel cartridges, module interfaces, sensor integration, cable management, and custom electronics."
        },
        {
          "label": "Key engineering challenge",
          "text": "The central challenge is fitting all of these systems into an FTC-scale robot without allowing modularity or serviceability to consume excessive space or compromise structural and drivetrain performance."
        },
        {
          "label": "What I learned",
          "text": "MK2 has pushed me to think less about individual parts and more about the dependencies between systems. Mechanical packaging, electronics, wiring, sensing, and maintenance all have to be designed together."
        }
      ],
